Add dedupe function and apply. Keep it simple.

```js
function dedupeOverlapping(words) {
  const lower = words.map((w) => w.toLowerCase());
  return words.filter((word, index) => {
    const current = lower[index];
    return !words.some((other, otherIndex) => {
      if (otherIndex === index) return false;
      const candidate = lower[otherIndex];
      return candidate.length < current.length && current.indexOf(candidate) !== -1;
    });
  });
}
```

But this would remove "red square" when "square" is present, and "blue circle" when "circle" is present. Result: cube? Let's trace:
- words = ["square","circle","red square","blue circle"]
- "square": is there a shorter word contained in "square"? No (circle no, red square longer, blue circle longer). Keep.
- "circle": keep.
- "red square": shorter "square" is contained → remove.
- "blue circle": shorter "circle" contained → remove.
Result: ["square","circle"]. Good.

If after dedupe < 2, fall back to clean. Then provider.

Also, dedupe exact duplicates first? The recognize provider already dedupes exact. But compose could receive duplicates. Add a Set-based dedupe.

Let me implement:
```js
function dedupeWords(words) {
  const seen = {};
  const unique = [];
  words.forEach((word) => {
    const key = word.toLowerCase();
    if (!seen[key]) { seen[key] = true; unique.push(word); }
  });
  const lower = unique.map((w) => w.toLowerCase());
