- `minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1` (known working)
- `English_Graceful_Lady`
- `Japanese_IntellectualSenior`
- `Japanese_KindLady`
- `Korean_SweetGirl`
- `Korean_CheerfulBoyfriend`
- `French_Female_News Anchor` (space may be invalid)
- `Spanish_SereneWoman`
- `German_FriendlyMan`
- Maybe MiniMax voice IDs for these languages from docs: `French_Male_News Anchor`, `Spanish_Female_News Anchor`, `German_Female_News Anchor`? I'm not sure.
Let me search the web for MiniMax system voice IDs. Use webfetch on the MiniMax docs or a search. The docs URL might be `https://platform.minimaxi.com/document/voice` or `https://www.minimaxi.com/document/voice`. Let me try fetching a search result. Alternatively, test candidates and see.

Since the tts function is deployed and accepts `voice`, I can write a test script in the simulator to loop through candidates and texts, calling tts, and record success/failure. Each call may take 2-5s; 10 candidates * 5s = 50s. The cloud function timeout is 20s; each call is separate. We can run them sequentially with delays, storing results in a global object. But automation_evaluate is synchronous; we can fire a chain and poll. Let's write a JS function that iterates a list sequentially, calling tts for each, and records `{voice, lang, ok, error, size}`. It writes audio files to USER_DATA_PATH. Then poll after ~60s. The evaluate returns "fired" immediately; the chain runs in the background.

Test cases:
```
[
 {lang:'en', text:'I can see a cat.', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','English_Graceful_Lady','English_Trustworth_Man']},
 {lang:'ja', text:'猫と犬が見えます。', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','Japanese_IntellectualSenior','Japanese_KindLady']},
 {lang:'ko', text:'고양이와 강아지가 보여요.', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','Korean_SweetGirl','Korean_CheerfulBoyfriend']},
 {lang:'fr', text:'Je vois un chat et un chien.', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','French_Female_News Anchor','French_Male_News Anchor']},
 {lang:'es', text:'Veo un gato y un perro.', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','Spanish_SereneWoman','Spanish_MaturePartner']},
 {lang:'de', text:'Ich sehe eine Katze und einen Hund.', voices:['minimax_55e81114-ce7e-4ca1','German_FriendlyMan','German_ConfidentWoman']},
]
```
That's 18 calls, ~2-5s each => 40-90s. The global variable records results. We'll poll after 90s. The evaluate returns immediately.

We should also save the audio for the user to listen. Each success writes `voice-test-<lang>-<index>.mp3`. We can then play them one by one? The user can listen. We can also return the file sizes and paths.

Note: The voice IDs with spaces may cause errors; that's okay, we'll see.

Let me run the test. Use `automation_evaluate` with the chain. The function source is long but manageable.

I'll build a JS function:
```js
function(){
  globalThis.__voiceTest = [];
  var cases = [...];
  var fs = wx.getFileSystemManager();
  var idx = 0;
  function next(){
    if (idx >= cases.length) { globalThis.__voiceTestDone = true; return; }
    var c = cases[idx++];
    var p = new Promise(function(resolve){ resolve(); });
    // sequential calls within a case:
    var vi = 0;
    function nextVoice(){
      if (vi >= c.voices.length) { next(); return; }
      var v = c.voices[vi++];
      wx.cloud.callFunction({name:'tts', data:{text:c.text, voice:v}}).then(function(r){
        var res=r.result;
        if(res && res.ok && res.data && res.data.audioBase64){
          var path = wx.env.USER_DATA_PATH + '/vt-' + c.lang + '-' + vi + '.mp3';
          fs.writeFileSync(path, res.data.audioBase64, 'base64');
          globalThis.__voiceTest.push({lang:c.lang, voice:v, ok:true, size:res.data.size, path:path});
        } else {
          globalThis.__voiceTest.push({lang:c.lang, voice:v, ok:false, err:JSON.stringify(res)});
        }
        nextVoice();
      }).catch(function(e){
        globalThis.__voiceTest.push({lang:c.lang, voice:v, ok:false, err:(e.errMsg||e.message)});
        nextVoice();
      });
    }
    nextVoice();
  }
  next();
  return 'fired';
}
```
This runs sequentially. The nested `next`/`nextVoice` functions are defined. Good.

